00:00:11.133 --> 00:00:13.866
[58* Part 1 Color/Sound 1972, Fall Univ. 25,
 Study 102

00:00:13.866 --> 00:00:17.532
McGraw-Hill Interview Scenes Include Last 
Survivors CA 5 minutes]

00:00:17.533 --> 00:00:22.366
[Bill Roberts:] Two and a half years ago, we 
found Dr. John Calhoun knee-deep in
mice,

00:00:22.366 --> 00:00:26.266
in the mouse heaven he designed for the
 National Institute of Mental Health

00:00:26.266 --> 00:00:28.099
Animal Center near Washington.

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:32.466
Dr. Calhoun wanted to find out what would
 happen to a mouse colony

00:00:32.466 --> 00:00:35.532
given everything mice need except living
 space.

00:00:35.533 --> 00:00:37.499
The result was chilling.

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:42.266
Two and a half years ago, more than two
 thousand mice were packed into Mouse
 City.

00:00:42.266 --> 00:00:47.166
Today, a tiny handful of survivors huddles in one
 corner of the city.

00:00:47.166 --> 00:00:52.199
Dr. Calhoun explains what the
 overcrowding did to Mouse City’s
inhabitants.

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:59.366
[Dr. John B. Calhoun:] The last thousand animals born never learned to develop the social behaviors.

00:00:59.366 --> 00:01:04.632
They never learned to be aggressive, which is 
necessary in defense of homesites.

00:01:04.633 --> 00:01:09.533
They never learned to court; there was no 
mating.

00:01:09.533 --> 00:01:17.099
Being no mating, there were no progeny
and the older animals uhh, whose behavior

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:23.733
was already becoming disrupted, they
 eventually reach to, an age too old to 
reproduce.

00:01:23.733 --> 00:01:31.166
And so it was all left up to these last 
thousand or so, who we call the beautiful
 ones,

00:01:31.166 --> 00:01:38.632
because, not engaging in any stressful 
activity and only paying attention to 
themselves,

00:01:38.633 --> 00:01:43.066
they groomed themselves well so they
 look uh, very fine specimen.

00:01:43.066 --> 00:01:50.399
But from that point on, which was about 
the time that you were here two and a half
 years ago,

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:55.933
this reproduction totally ceased and the 
animals have just aged and died.

00:01:55.933 --> 00:02:01.766
[Roberts:] At least one human group, the 
Ik tribe discovered in Africa by Dr. Colin 
Turnbull,

00:02:01.766 --> 00:02:06.332
has gone through a similar, terrible
 process, according to Dr. Calhoun.

00:02:06.333 --> 00:02:12.233
[Dr. Calhoun:] The two conditions that they
 went through was that there was a
 decision

00:02:12.233 --> 00:02:19.199
on the then-colonial government to make
 a wildlife preserve of this big valley

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:22.233
which was their home, they’re
 hunter-gatherers.

00:02:22.233 --> 00:02:28.766
They were moved up onto the mountain
side and gathered together in much higher
 density.

00:02:28.766 --> 00:02:33.599
Instead of being spread out in small 
groups, they were all placed in one place,

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:37.800
and also required to engage in an entirely different way of life,

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:40.800
in which their social roles, the whole...

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:44.800
everything they'd learned how to do had 
no meaning.

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:50.200
Under those conditions, the social 
behavior broke down.

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:54.200
They began living, they still lived together

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:59.233
but their activities were directed towards
 themselves.

00:02:59.233 --> 00:03:02.499
Everything you would think that’s human 
disappeared.

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:08.166
[Roberts:] Perhaps the most chilling
 conclusion from his Mouse City studies,
 says Dr. Calhoun,

00:03:08.166 --> 00:03:14.799
is that once this change in social behavior 
occurs, it apparently cannot be reversed.

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:23.066
[Dr. Calhoun:] It's this fact of apparent 
irreversible phase shifts that gives some
 of us

00:03:23.066 --> 00:03:26.699
particular concern over the fate of the human situation.

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:35.166
Because we see worldwide, the breaking
 down of social roles, the increase in
 contact

00:03:35.166 --> 00:03:39.899
will result in the breakdown of social roles, because there are too many people.

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:47.100
This might lead to an overriding of our
 controls of our total culture and would be

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:53.833
set into a process which would either fix
us rigidly forever into some sort of

00:03:53.833 --> 00:04:00.199
global tribalism without any adaptation 
to change,

00:04:00.200 --> 00:04:07.066
or there would result, perhaps eventually,
 some decline very much like we’ve seen
 here,

00:04:07.066 --> 00:04:10.499
but postponed somewhat.

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:17.666
[Roberts:] Dr. John B. Calhoun, with a very
 sobering look at a possible future for us.

00:04:17.666 --> 00:04:24.466
Bill Roberts from the National Institute of 
Mental Health Animal Center near 
Washington.

00:04:24.466 --> 00:04:29.266
[59* Part 1(CONTD) Color 1970 April 1 ca
 UDN:892 Univ. 25, Study 102,

00:04:29.266 --> 00:04:35.799
Carl Coleman, Time-Life Interview, CA 30 
Minutes]

00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:46.000
[Background sounds and voices]

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:50.066
[Carl Coleman:] Dr. Calhoun, why do we have
 to make decisions now

00:04:50.066 --> 00:04:52.699
on our population control?

00:04:52.700 --> 00:04:56.466
[Dr. Calhoun:] Well, the population's a 
very interesting one.

00:04:56.466 --> 00:04:59.866
There’s a lot of talk about a population 
explosion.

00:04:59.866 --> 00:05:04.866
Well, there's no population explosion, 
at least not quite yet, and never has been,

00:05:04.866 --> 00:05:10.332
or you might say there's always been a 
population explosion.

00:05:10.333 --> 00:05:15.066
If we go back to about forty thousand 
years ago, uhh there were perhaps

00:05:15.066 --> 00:05:17.832
four and a half million people on earth.

00:05:17.833 --> 00:05:21.466
It had been not...many fewer than this 
for the previous

00:05:21.466 --> 00:05:25.099
two million years of man's evolution.

00:05:25.100 --> 00:05:29.133
And then suddenly the numbers began 
to increase,

00:05:29.133 --> 00:05:35.199
and perhaps a little crude drawing here 
will uhh show this.

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:38.266
These are in millions now.

00:05:38.266 --> 00:05:47.966
And this is one, ten, a hundred million, one billion, and we go up above here.

00:05:47.966 --> 00:05:56.599
And if we take a time scale here, an
 inverse log, we go back to a hundred
 thousand years ago.

00:05:56.600 --> 00:06:01.133
And we go back to, we’ll make
 this then to ten thousand years ago,

00:06:01.133 --> 00:06:08.066
to one thousand years ago, one 
thousand years ago.

00:06:08.066 --> 00:06:16.099
Then a hundred which is one-tenth,
to one year, from sometime in the future.

00:06:16.100 --> 00:06:23.500
So these are in time, in thousands of 
years, and if we look at population growth

00:06:23.500 --> 00:06:28.733
in this way, starting back about forty 
thousand years ago...

00:06:28.733 --> 00:06:36.766
population starting at about here, has
 gone roughly in this direction.

00:06:36.766 --> 00:06:44.766
Now this is a rather complex curve, it’s the 
Von Foerster curve, but simply said,
 starting here

00:06:44.766 --> 00:06:52.866
at forty thousand years ago, it took about 
twenty thousand years, umm about twenty thousand years

00:06:52.866 --> 00:06:56.232
for the population to double.

00:06:56.233 --> 00:07:01.399
Then the next doubling required about ten thousand years.

00:07:01.400 --> 00:07:06.866
Then each doubling, five thousand years 'til the 
next doubling, twenty-five hundred and so on.

00:07:06.866 --> 00:07:08.499
[Coleman:] What doubling are we in 
now, Doctor?

00:07:08.500 --> 00:07:13.133
[Dr. Calhoun:] We’re in the tenth doubling 
and the eleventh doubling.

00:07:13.133 --> 00:07:15.233
[Coleman:] Is that the final one?

00:07:15.233 --> 00:07:18.099
[Dr. Calhoun:] Well pretty near, it might be.

00:07:18.100 --> 00:07:27.366
The final doubling, starting shortly after the next, way up here.

00:07:27.366 --> 00:07:35.632
We’re up in here, scale about right, uhh in 
which the world population might be of the 
order

00:07:35.633 --> 00:07:44.199
of four and a half billion now, people, would
 require only twenty years to double.

00:07:44.200 --> 00:07:48.766
So starting from twenty thousand years for 
the first doubling up to about twenty
 years,

00:07:48.766 --> 00:07:53.199
if this historical process continues.

00:07:53.200 --> 00:07:58.266
So we’re back here a little ways from that,
 not very far from early in the next century.

00:07:58.266 --> 00:08:03.299
[Coleman:] Can it be mathematically 
projected, Doctor, on a calendar year 
perhaps,

00:08:03.300 --> 00:08:08.366
when the population reaches the point 
where it outstrips our natural resources,

00:08:08.366 --> 00:08:11.566
our power supplies, our food supplies.

00:08:11.566 --> 00:08:15.732
[Dr. Calhoun:] When you say
 mathematically this is what Von Foerster 
did...

00:08:15.733 --> 00:08:21.833
Projecting this age-old trend, not age-old, 
forty thousand years, rather short time.

00:08:21.833 --> 00:08:26.433
If this trend continued, you would have 
instantaneous doubling, but we know

00:08:26.433 --> 00:08:32.599
that's impossible, and thus the critical 
question is that something is going to 
happen,

00:08:32.600 --> 00:08:36.300
that man will be different from the start of
man.

00:08:36.300 --> 00:08:40.433
And this is where man started about forty
 thousand years ago as a cultural animal.

00:08:40.433 --> 00:08:46.133
Not as a biological animal, he went 
considerably back in time below that.

00:08:46.133 --> 00:08:50.399
But if beginning this new process 
where he quote, now is the only animal

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:54.666
who could really control his numbers,
 that is to keep enlarging them,

00:08:54.666 --> 00:08:58.632
which is increasing his own potentiality by
 so doing.

00:08:58.633 --> 00:09:04.933
And this is continued until now we hit a
 critical point here, where this process 
can’t continue.

00:09:04.933 --> 00:09:11.333
And so from this time forty thousand years 
ago, to early in the next century,

00:09:11.333 --> 00:09:15.233
defines what we call man.

00:09:15.233 --> 00:09:20.266
And man ends at this point, 'cause there 
are a lot of bodies around,

00:09:20.266 --> 00:09:24.432
he’s going to have to be something quite
 different than what he has been 
historically.

00:09:24.433 --> 00:09:28.666
And so one thing that he could do and
 we’re certainly capable

00:09:28.666 --> 00:09:31.199
of doing it, is keep reproducing.

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:34.500
So that’s one choice.

00:09:34.500 --> 00:09:39.200
And another choice that we have, 
early in the, which will have to be in effect

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:44.566
by early in the next century, is if he can stabilize the population.

00:09:44.566 --> 00:09:48.732
One, he can keep going not very much
 longer.

00:09:48.733 --> 00:09:52.733
Above nine, well I haven’t got this, see.

00:09:52.733 --> 00:09:59.833
This would have been at nine billion, I 
haven’t quite gotten my figures here to 
double to nine billion.

00:09:59.833 --> 00:10:09.599
This is the end point if we, if the number 
of children per adults

00:10:09.600 --> 00:10:11.866
continues as it's been in the past,

00:10:11.866 --> 00:10:17.599
it would be about nine billion people 
at this point.

00:10:17.600 --> 00:10:24.133
If we begin slowing down so that the 
population can stabilize, which is the 
ecological model

00:10:24.133 --> 00:10:30.166
as we’ve tried to for wildlife management 
to maintain a stable population,

00:10:30.166 --> 00:10:38.232
then, with fewer children, we might keep 
the, at this level we have seven and a half 
billion.

00:10:38.233 --> 00:10:41.966
Another critical point here is how many adults.

00:10:41.966 --> 00:10:47.866
Ideally uhh, there should be, at this upper 
optimum,

00:10:47.866 --> 00:10:51.599
three and a half billion adults in order to 
make most effective

00:10:51.600 --> 00:10:56.300
the network of people communicating with 
people.

00:10:56.300 --> 00:10:59.966
However, if we go on the third, there's a
 third choice,

00:10:59.966 --> 00:11:05.532
and this is the population will begin slowly 
to decline.

00:11:05.533 --> 00:11:10.599
And if we go towards that goal, we would 
have at that point

00:11:10.600 --> 00:11:13.500
roughly about 6.3 billion total people.

00:11:13.500 --> 00:11:18.066
With less than, considerably less than two 
children per family.

00:11:18.066 --> 00:11:21.466
[Coleman:] Doctor what do you think now of the stress of our environmental
 problems,

00:11:21.466 --> 00:11:22.866
trying to solve them?

00:11:22.866 --> 00:11:28.199
Should government, the politicians pay 
more attention to this problem, do you 
think?

00:11:28.200 --> 00:11:31.233
They’re all interlaced, it isn’t that simple.

00:11:31.233 --> 00:11:36.466
[Dr. Calhoun:] Well, the problem, the 
environment is just one problem

00:11:36.466 --> 00:11:42.266
which we’re focused on now, and this may
be a real trap because we only look

00:11:42.266 --> 00:11:47.199
at the physical, biological aspects of our
 surroundings.

00:11:47.200 --> 00:11:53.833
Where if we focused instead, on what is a 
much more essential comprehensive
 problem,

00:11:53.833 --> 00:11:58.066
and this is evolution and the designing of 
evolution.

00:11:58.066 --> 00:12:04.332
In fact, we like to say that, uhh, we are 
revolutionists here,

00:12:04.333 --> 00:12:08.066
but a particular kind of revolutionist that 
you could now spell differently.

00:12:08.066 --> 00:12:13.132
Now we all know what this symbol is, this is prescription, but uhh,

00:12:13.133 --> 00:12:16.066
[Sound of chalk writing on chalkboard]

00:12:16.066 --> 00:12:21.199
so we are concerned with the prescription
 for evolution of the design of the future,

00:12:21.200 --> 00:12:24.933
the design of evolution. So we’re
 revolutionists.

00:12:24.933 --> 00:12:29.899
[Murmuring in background]

00:12:29.900 --> 00:12:35.866
[Coleman:] Well doctor, why are we falling
 into a trap, paying so much attention to 
environment?

00:12:35.866 --> 00:12:42.066
[Dr. Calhoun:] Well. We are in an 
environmental crisis, there’s no question 
about it.

00:12:42.066 --> 00:12:45.799
The concern of the government in this
 question is good.

00:12:45.800 --> 00:12:50.200
The even greater concern, particularly at 
the college level

00:12:50.200 --> 00:12:55.333
and the environmental teach-ins that 
are underway during April of this year

00:12:55.333 --> 00:13:02.133
are extremely good movements, but they
may be a very deathly trap.

00:13:02.133 --> 00:13:07.966
For this reason, that is, if we can focus
 just on the good environment,

00:13:07.966 --> 00:13:15.632
the biological environment, the physical
 environment, we will miss a great deal of 
what man is or could be.

00:13:15.633 --> 00:13:21.033
With a concern for increasing human
 potentiality, and again we like to say

00:13:21.033 --> 00:13:35.366
we’d like to put mental, as in human capacity,
 back in environmental.

00:13:35.366 --> 00:13:38.199
And this is what we’re all about...

00:13:38.200 --> 00:13:41.466
and we could set a trap, see.

00:13:41.466 --> 00:13:46.166
The trap we’re setting here is we’re 
looking at the environment on the

00:13:46.166 --> 00:13:52.932
strictly biological model and not a man-
future evolutionary-directed model.

00:13:52.933 --> 00:13:59.499
If we can develop a stable population 
in which everything becomes fixed,

00:13:59.500 --> 00:14:05.666
it’ll be unchanging over time, and now
 we’ll be back in a global tribal situation.

00:14:05.666 --> 00:14:09.932
Sure we might survive for millions of
years, but we aren’t going anywhere,

00:14:09.933 --> 00:14:16.466
we’re like the lungfish in Lake Manyara,
 who in this same situation,

00:14:16.466 --> 00:14:18.066
they’ve handled it very effectively.

00:14:18.066 --> 00:14:22.766
Whenever stresses come along, they
 encapsulate themselves in a mud ball

00:14:22.766 --> 00:14:25.832
and wait out the dry period and then come 
back when the environment

00:14:25.833 --> 00:14:28.466
is back in a suitable condition.

00:14:28.466 --> 00:14:33.666
So we could become lungfish and
 evolution would essentially end.

00:14:33.666 --> 00:14:36.832
[Coleman:] What is government or what should government be doing now?

00:14:36.833 --> 00:14:41.599
Is it aware of this problem in your opinion, are 
we doing enough?

00:14:41.600 --> 00:14:43.466
We’re doing very little, aren’t we?

00:14:43.466 --> 00:14:47.566
[Dr. Calhoun:] I think there’s not, I don’t 
think it's necessarily leveled at the
 government.

00:14:47.566 --> 00:14:54.466
I think this is a, whatever comment that
 could be made here is one of general 
awareness,

00:14:54.466 --> 00:14:58.566
of becoming involved in the design of
evolution,

00:14:58.566 --> 00:15:01.032
the prescription for further evolution.

00:15:01.033 --> 00:15:07.033
We have to have a long-time perspective,
 both in the past and the future,

00:15:07.033 --> 00:15:10.799
and where we may go.

00:15:10.800 --> 00:15:18.533
And that, that the issue, the big crisis now,
is not when we think of environment

00:15:18.533 --> 00:15:19.699
relating to number of people.

00:15:19.700 --> 00:15:21.666
It's not number of people.

00:15:21.666 --> 00:15:29.266
It's people times capacities of the average
 individual or the total human capacity.

00:15:29.266 --> 00:15:36.566
Now if we, we can choose, now
 is the time for choosing a goal.

00:15:36.566 --> 00:15:39.066
And this in part relates to numbers.

00:15:39.066 --> 00:15:44.099
But it also pertains to whether we
are going to select, from what has been

00:15:44.100 --> 00:15:46.666
the definition of man for the last
 forty thousand years,

00:15:46.666 --> 00:15:51.966
some aspect of this, one of these is what 
we call human potentiality,

00:15:51.966 --> 00:15:57.432
the capacities of the average individual to 
understand, to develop new insights,

00:15:57.433 --> 00:16:00.099
to communicate with his fellows more 
effectively.

00:16:00.100 --> 00:16:06.100
If that is a major overriding goal and is 
selected, and we select,

00:16:06.100 --> 00:16:10.966
and one of the necessities for that is 
maintaining a changing environment,

00:16:10.966 --> 00:16:16.032
which includes the numbers, and the only 
way to do that now is to decrease 
numbers.

00:16:16.033 --> 00:16:21.333
So that if we choose the goal of gradually 
going into declining population,

00:16:21.333 --> 00:16:28.099
with increasing human potentiality, we’re
 now at 1970.

00:16:28.100 --> 00:16:31.900
The critical point is about seventy-five 
years from now.

00:16:31.900 --> 00:16:35.633
There's tremendous time lag in social 
change.

00:16:35.633 --> 00:16:40.933
I mean it's been only a very short time
since the Supreme Court ruling for 
integration

00:16:40.933 --> 00:16:45.499
and we’ve made lots of strides in some
 ways and very little in others.

00:16:45.500 --> 00:16:49.333
But this is a much, much more drastic
 change,

00:16:49.333 --> 00:16:54.533
and if you’re going to change numbers
 towards declining population,

00:16:54.533 --> 00:17:00.699
the whole attitude of people’s values, the
 decisions, have to be made in a very short 
time.

00:17:00.700 --> 00:17:06.333
We’ve got, let’s say fifteen years, or 
to 1984.

00:17:06.333 --> 00:17:17.433
So in essence if we, we have about fifteen
 years of grace or opportunity

00:17:17.433 --> 00:17:19.499
that this crisis presents to us,

00:17:19.500 --> 00:17:23.133
in which we can change our whole value
 system, or not change the value system

00:17:23.133 --> 00:17:26.299
but change the mechanism for changing
 the value system.

00:17:26.300 --> 00:17:29.766
It’s a meta-process.

00:17:29.766 --> 00:17:35.032
If we don’t do it within this time, we will
 probably be at, we will go into 1984...

00:17:35.033 --> 00:17:37.933
[Coleman:] George Orwell.
[Dr. Calhoun:] in the Orwellian sense.

00:17:37.933 --> 00:17:40.099
So this is why it's critical.

00:17:40.100 --> 00:17:46.600
It’s the most, by this time, right now is 
the most critical time since sometime

00:17:46.600 --> 00:17:48.866
before when man made this discovery

00:17:48.866 --> 00:17:52.099
which probably took him about fifteen
 thousand years

00:17:52.100 --> 00:17:57.633
to learn how to discover a new kind 
of space, a conceptual world of ideas

00:17:57.633 --> 00:18:01.333
and progressive development of ideas.

00:18:01.333 --> 00:18:06.433
So that in the early primitive hunter group
 they had about fifteen thousand years

00:18:06.433 --> 00:18:13.333
to solve their impasse of when they were
 stuck by the carrying capacity of
 environment,

00:18:13.333 --> 00:18:14.666
the physical environment.

00:18:14.666 --> 00:18:18.966
Now we’re also stuck with a similar problem 
and have about fifteen years.

00:18:18.966 --> 00:18:21.232
So it's quite a burden.

00:18:21.233 --> 00:18:25.999
And the future, the entire future of
 evolution depends on the next fifteen
 years.

00:18:26.000 --> 00:18:31.166
The people here now, who are involved 
now...

00:18:31.166 --> 00:18:37.632
which is critical and uh, it’s a question of
 involvement.

00:18:37.633 --> 00:18:46.733
I’m an ecologist, ecologist/psychologist,
 however hybrid, and it’s a question of

00:18:46.733 --> 00:18:53.433
environment with involvement in the total
 environment of physical and social,

00:18:53.433 --> 00:18:56.399
that is the crucial question in our studies
 here

00:18:56.400 --> 00:19:00.066
which we use mice and rats and other
 animals for.

00:19:00.066 --> 00:19:10.032
And that’s where we bring into focus our 
research, is on the question of
 involvement.

00:19:10.033 --> 00:19:15.866
And they’re bitten up and the one that's 
bitten fails to run away.

00:19:15.866 --> 00:19:18.066
There's one type of..

00:19:18.066 --> 00:19:19.699
[Interviewer:] Okay doctor.

00:19:19.700 --> 00:19:24.433
[Dr. Calhoun:] And this is one of the 
types of lack of involvement,

00:19:24.433 --> 00:19:29.566
of total withdrawal from the reality 
of the environment,

00:19:29.566 --> 00:19:33.199
that’s compatible with that species.

00:19:33.200 --> 00:19:36.066
[Interviewer:] What, twenty-four hundred 
mice in this enclosure?

00:19:36.066 --> 00:19:38.866
[Dr. Calhoun:] There’s something of the
 order, we don’t know exactly what it is
now.

00:19:38.866 --> 00:19:41.999
It’s between twenty-two hundred and 
twenty-five hundred mice,

00:19:42.000 --> 00:19:44.800
that is bodies, moving bodies.

00:19:44.800 --> 00:19:49.800
But there are really only uhh about a hundred
 and fifty mice in here.

00:19:49.800 --> 00:19:53.333
Real mice, that is mice who are in the 
social swim.

00:19:53.333 --> 00:19:58.299
All the others have been rejected in one
 way or another.

00:19:58.300 --> 00:20:02.566
[Interviewer:] What’s happened…

00:20:02.566 --> 00:20:06.699
[Dr. Calhoun:] …reproductive behavior's
 completely inhibited.

00:20:06.700 --> 00:20:13.766
It’s inhibited because the la…it's inhibited
 because the last few hundred...

00:20:13.766 --> 00:20:18.766
maintained them as juveniles and
 although they are now adults uh they no
 longer

00:20:18.766 --> 00:20:23.699
engage in aggressive behavior or in 
reproductive behavior.

00:20:23.700 --> 00:20:28.033
And we call them the beautiful ones because 
they are so excellent physically,

00:20:28.033 --> 00:20:29.899
they are unstressed.

00:20:29.900 --> 00:20:31.233
Now I see one down here.

00:20:31.233 --> 00:20:35.366
Most of these live in the apartment houses 
and rarely come out, but I see one

00:20:35.366 --> 00:20:39.532
who's taken temporary refuge in one of
 the nesting material supply cans.

00:20:39.533 --> 00:20:44.699
I’ll go over and pull him out, so that you 
can see what a beautiful specimen this
 animal is.

00:20:44.700 --> 00:20:47.000
[Interviewer:] But as far as the mouse 
is concerned he’s a nothing?

00:20:47.000 --> 00:20:50.966
[Dr. Calhoun:] He’s a total nothing.

00:20:50.966 --> 00:21:01.432
[Calhoun climbs into the enclosure.]

00:21:01.433 --> 00:21:05.866
Now here he is lying, in the uh...you
 going?

00:21:05.866 --> 00:21:07.166
[Interviewer:] Go ahead doctor.

00:21:07.166 --> 00:21:10.099
[Dr. Calhoun:] Here he is lying in the can.

00:21:10.100 --> 00:21:14.466
It’s a place of temporary retreat.

00:21:14.466 --> 00:21:18.866
He’s colored very few, practically no wounds,
 he’s in excellent physical shape.

00:21:18.866 --> 00:21:24.899
He has two or three nicks but compared to most
 of the animals, he’s in excellent shape.

00:21:24.900 --> 00:21:31.033
And these animals from previous study shows 
that they are, are unstressed.

00:21:31.033 --> 00:21:34.699
They’re just not here insofar as
involvement.

00:21:34.700 --> 00:21:38.033
[Interviewer:] You started this
 experiment with just a few mice

00:21:38.033 --> 00:21:42.099
some twenty months ago...apparently
 you’ve been able to dissect some?

00:21:42.100 --> 00:21:45.700
Have you noticed any changes in tissue?

00:21:45.700 --> 00:21:48.333
[Dr. Calhoun:] Well the major thing...
[Interviewer:] The stress.

00:21:48.333 --> 00:21:52.366
[Dr. Calhoun:] The major thing, well
 certain classes of these, for instance

00:21:52.366 --> 00:21:55.899
comparing these males on the floor here 
who are withdrawn.

00:21:55.900 --> 00:21:59.366
They are highly stressed animals but
 the stress comes from each other

00:21:59.366 --> 00:22:03.066
because of the peculiar violence that they
 exhibit.

00:22:03.066 --> 00:22:11.899
Which leaves them as this animal, with
 his tail all chewed up, but they do it to 
each other.

00:22:11.900 --> 00:22:14.733
These are highly stressed individuals.

00:22:14.733 --> 00:22:18.099
On the other hand, the beautiful one 
which I just showed you,

00:22:18.100 --> 00:22:21.000
are very, extremely unstressed.

00:22:21.000 --> 00:22:29.133
We've run the, Dr. Julius Axelrod has run the
 assay of the enzyme which converts

00:22:29.133 --> 00:22:32.999
noradrenaline to adrenaline as the basis 
for this.

00:22:33.000 --> 00:22:39.200
On the other hand, we’ve been removing
 females from other populations just 
terminated

00:22:39.200 --> 00:22:46.433
and looking at their uteri, and for animals
 up to a year of age who’ve come up in the 
latter history

00:22:46.433 --> 00:22:51.633
of the colonies, there’s essentially no 
evidence of conceiving.

00:22:51.633 --> 00:22:56.866
So they’ve, at this stage, this is part of 
the over-living phenomenon.

00:22:56.866 --> 00:23:01.532
And it's these individuals who are not
 conceiving are the ones which are not 
stressed.

00:23:01.533 --> 00:23:04.666
They’re the beautiful ones who lack involvement.

00:23:04.666 --> 00:23:10.932
So that’s the uh, the essential story of 
what’s going on in our studies.

00:23:10.933 --> 00:23:13.966
[Interviewer:] Have you been surprised 
Doctor, about the results of this,

00:23:13.966 --> 00:23:15.399
what you’ve learned so far?

00:23:15.400 --> 00:23:19.333
Some twenty months ago did you think all
 these things were going to happen?

00:23:19.333 --> 00:23:23.033
[Dr. Calhoun:] Well, I had a slightly 
different hypothesis

00:23:23.033 --> 00:23:27.166
which held true only in the early stages,

00:23:27.166 --> 00:23:34.266
and this was that the larger the space the 
uh, smaller the ultimate population
 density.

00:23:34.266 --> 00:23:36.366
That’s proved not to be true,

00:23:36.366 --> 00:23:41.432
but a second aspect of that hypothesis
was that the larger the space with 
reference

00:23:41.433 --> 00:23:44.533
to the number of colonizers, the slower it would
 get started.

00:23:44.533 --> 00:23:50.566
This has been true because the essential
 analogy here is that when there are few 
animals

00:23:50.566 --> 00:23:57.866
as in this particular universe, a male, any 
mouse when he was out would rarely
 meet another.

00:23:57.866 --> 00:24:03.299
Therefore, he identified with all of 
his physical environment.

00:24:03.300 --> 00:24:06.966
So his own ego boundaries extended out 
to include all of these things

00:24:06.966 --> 00:24:09.232
which he was committed to,

00:24:09.233 --> 00:24:14.066
and therefore, any other animal coming
 out and moving towards any portion of
that

00:24:14.066 --> 00:24:19.099
would elicit anxiety and therefore
 aggression.

00:24:19.100 --> 00:24:24.066
And we have huge territories and this 
is an essential formulation of territoriality.

00:24:24.066 --> 00:24:28.432
So that in the larger the space, the more 
likely animals develop territoriality,

00:24:28.433 --> 00:24:29.699
the more extreme.

00:24:29.700 --> 00:24:39.066
And this did inhibit initial reproductive rate 
and so with a smaller, we have smaller

00:24:39.066 --> 00:24:42.066
universes than this and they grew much faster.

00:24:42.066 --> 00:24:43.766
They got started quicker.

00:24:43.766 --> 00:24:50.199
So that’s another facet of this problem, that 
that gradually washed out beyond a
 certain level,

00:24:50.200 --> 00:24:59.366
and uh then the population increased for a
 while, fairly rapidly, and then it hit this 
level at which animals

00:24:59.366 --> 00:25:05.799
begin being rejected out into public space, 
as the males living on the floor here.

00:25:05.800 --> 00:25:09.600
The females are rejected but they just 
don’t come out,

00:25:09.600 --> 00:25:11.666
they stay back in the apartment houses.

00:25:11.666 --> 00:25:15.699
[Coleman:] Doctor, one final question 
here, as a human…

00:25:15.700 --> 00:25:18.633
[Murmuring]

00:25:18.633 --> 00:25:22.966
Dr. Calhoun, as a human being, after
 going through this experiment,

00:25:22.966 --> 00:25:24.366
what goes through your mind?

00:25:24.366 --> 00:25:28.532
Are you worried about the future of mankind?

00:25:28.533 --> 00:25:34.299
[Dr. Calhoun:] A pathological situation like 
this gives us an understanding of what 
may be going on

00:25:34.300 --> 00:25:39.666
in the human situation, and what might go 
on, and be catastrophic.

00:25:39.666 --> 00:25:43.866
And what is really catastrophic and where 
the real fear is,

00:25:43.866 --> 00:25:48.999
is on a level where the mice and man are 
extremely similar.

00:25:49.000 --> 00:25:54.266
That is, the mice have programs, they're 
like little computers, uh,

00:25:54.266 --> 00:25:59.832
and this is basically genetic with a much
smaller element of learned behavior.

00:25:59.833 --> 00:26:08.166
And too-high a contact rate disorganizes 
the program so that it gets washed out.

00:26:08.166 --> 00:26:11.332
So it’s disconnected or it doesn’t express 
itself.

00:26:11.333 --> 00:26:13.966
This is where you get the lack of involvement.

00:26:13.966 --> 00:26:20.066
On a human level, we do have a much 
more complex brain, it does get
 programmed,

00:26:20.066 --> 00:26:25.932
less by genetics, less by heredity than for 
the mice, much more cultural,

00:26:25.933 --> 00:26:28.233
but it’s just as subject to being
 disorganized,

00:26:28.233 --> 00:26:35.566
to having a program disorganized through 
inappropriate sequences of contact,

00:26:35.566 --> 00:26:37.766
too many contacts.

00:26:37.766 --> 00:26:42.932
And so that we have this danger and part 
of the danger

00:26:42.933 --> 00:26:46.899
is that we don’t have an adequate 
establishment.

00:26:46.900 --> 00:26:48.066
Not a...

00:26:48.066 --> 00:26:54.532
We’re now seeing, because the, quote, 
establishment doesn’t provide

00:26:54.533 --> 00:26:57.166
all appropriate channels, we should
 destroy the establishment.

00:26:57.166 --> 00:27:02.799
We need better establishment to allow
 interrelationships to protect us from too 
many contacts,

00:27:02.800 --> 00:27:08.000
but to allow us for those contacts which 
we do engage in to be more meaningful

00:27:08.000 --> 00:27:09.833
for our own lives,

00:27:09.833 --> 00:27:15.633
and for, meaningful to others in a
 compassionate sense of each of us

00:27:15.633 --> 00:27:19.733
being involved in helping the other to fulfill 
his role.

00:27:19.733 --> 00:27:21.766
We’ll have many more roles,

00:27:21.766 --> 00:27:25.099
and this is where the notion of
 compassion comes in,

00:27:25.100 --> 00:27:31.733
in helping, each of us helping others to 
fulfill their programs for development.

00:27:31.733 --> 00:27:39.333
And so, we’re at this peculiar stage where 
you’re highly optimistic, at the same time 
highly pessimistic,

00:27:39.333 --> 00:27:48.433
and you wish to throw your weight, intellectual weight, to moving towards the 
optimistic side,

00:27:48.433 --> 00:27:53.566
to opening up continuing evolution.

00:27:53.566 --> 00:27:57.666
